The Matlab code "EPYC_square_periodic.m" numerically solves the 2D continuum model of unphosphorylated (sticky) and phosphorylated (non-sticky) EPYC1 in a geometry mimicking the shape of
the Chlamydomonas chloroplast, using finite differences with periodic boundary conditions. The system follows a three-component Flory–Huggins
free energy describing EPYC1 and solvent interactions, and the switching rates between the two EPYC1 states are spatially uniform and depend
only on time.

The Matlab code "simp_condensate.m" solves the nonlinear ODE given by Eq. (15) in the Supplementary Material.
